.Dd October 19, 2026
.Dt EROFS 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm erofs
.Nd Enhanced Read-Only File System
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To compile this driver into the kernel, place the following line in
your kernel configuration file:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "options EROFS"
.Ed
.Pp
Alternatively, to load the driver as a module at boot time, place the
following line in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
erofs_load="YES"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver provides read-only access to file systems in the Enhanced Read-Only
File System (EROFS) format.
EROFS was designed for Linux and is used for system images, firmware and
container layers.
Images are normally created with
.Sy mkfs.erofs
from the erofs-utils package.
.Pp
The image can be on a disk provider or in a regular file.
A regular file can be mounted directly or through
.Xr mdconfig 8 .
The driver always mounts the file system read-only; an explicit
.Fl o Cm rw
still results in a read-only mount.
Operations that would modify the file system fail with
.Er EROFS .
.Pp
The driver understands compact and extended inodes, plain, inline
(tail-packed) and chunk-based data, compressed data, shared, inline and
long-prefix extended attributes, fragments, the metabox, 48-bit block counts,
and the device table of multi-device images.
Compressed data can use LZ4, DEFLATE, zstd or LZMA (MicroLZMA).
DEFLATE images are decoded with the kernel zlib module, which is loaded as a
dependency.
zstd images need a kernel with
.Cd "options ZSTDIO" ;
without it they are rejected when mounted.
Unsupported features of an image, such as an unknown data layout or
compression algorithm, are reported with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.Pp
Extended attributes in the user namespace are shown without a prefix.
Extended attributes in the system namespace keep their full names
.Pq Li trusted.* , security.* .
Linux POSIX access and default ACLs are converted to
.Fx
POSIX.1e ACLs.
This needs a kernel with
.Cd "options UFS_ACL" ;
without it, ACLs are not available, permissions are checked against the
mode bits only, and the
.Cm acl
and
.Cm noacl
mount options are rejected with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
The file system can be exported over NFS.
.Pp
There is no file system specific mount utility.
Use
.Xr mount 8
with
.Fl t Cm erofs ,
which passes the options below to
.Xr nmount 2 .
.Sh MOUNT OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm acl
Enable POSIX ACLs.
This is the default when the kernel has
.Cd "options UFS_ACL" .
.It Cm noacl
Do not use POSIX ACLs; check permissions against the mode bits only.
.It Cm user_xattr
Make the extended attributes of the user namespace available.
This is the default.
.It Cm nouser_xattr
Hide the extended attributes of the user namespace.
Attributes of the other namespaces and POSIX ACLs are not affected.
.It Cm directio
When the image is a regular file, read it with
.Dv IO_DIRECT ,
which asks the file system holding the image to bypass its buffer cache.
That file system may refuse the request or ignore it.
.It Cm nodirectio
Read the image through the page cache of the file holding it.
This is the default.
.It Cm fsoffset Ns = Ns Ar bytes
The file system starts
.Ar bytes
bytes into the device or file.
The offset must be a multiple of the block size of the image.
The default is 0.
.It Cm device Ns = Ns Ar path
Use the disk provider or file at
.Ar path
as the single external device of the image.
.It Cm device\&. Ns Ar N Ns = Ns Ar path
Use the disk provider or file at
.Ar path
as the external device in slot
.Ar N
(counting from 1) of the image.
.Xr mount 8
keeps only one of several options with the same name, so an image with more
than one external device needs the numbered form.
The two forms cannot be mixed.
Several slots may refer to the same path, including the path of the primary
device.
If the image has a device table and no external devices are given, the
additional devices are read from the primary device at the addresses recorded
in the table.
.It Cm dir_ra_bytes Ns = Ns Ar bytes
How much of a directory is read ahead when it is scanned.
The value must be 0, which turns readahead off, or a multiple of the block size
of the image, and at most the smaller of 1 MiB and 256 blocks.
The default is 16384.
.It Cm cache_strategy Ns = Ns Ar strategy
Decoded extents of compressed files are kept in a small cache.
With
.Cm disabled
the cache is not used.
.Cm readahead
and
.Cm readaround
are accepted for compatibility with Linux and currently behave in the same
way.
The default is
.Cm readaround .
.It Cm sync_decompress Ns = Ns Ar mode
Where compressed data is decoded.
With
.Cm auto
or
.Cm on
the thread that reads the data decodes it.
With
.Cm off
the work is handed to a pool of kernel threads, named
.Dq erofs decode ,
and the reading thread waits for the result.
.Cm 0 ,
.Cm 1
and
.Cm 2
are accepted for
.Cm auto ,
.Cm on
and
.Cm off .
The default is
.Cm auto .
.It Cm worker_queue_priority Ns = Ns Ar priority
The priority, from 0 to 255, of decoding tasks in the queue of the decode
threads.
Tasks with a larger value are run first; tasks with the same value are run in
the order they were queued.
The default is 0.
.It Cm async , noatime , noclusterr , noclusterw , nosymfollow , sync , union
Accepted so that a mount command written for another file system works.
They are handled by the VFS layer.
Only
.Cm nosymfollow
and
.Cm union
have an effect on a read-only file system.
.El
.Pp
These options can be changed with
.Cm mount -u :
.Cm acl ,
.Cm noacl ,
.Cm user_xattr ,
.Cm nouser_xattr ,
.Cm directio ,
.Cm nodirectio ,
.Cm dir_ra_bytes ,
.Cm cache_strategy ,
.Cm sync_decompress
and
.Cm worker_queue_priority .
.Cm fsoffset
can be given again only with its current value.
.Cm device
and
.Cm device\&. Ns Ar N
are rejected with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
because the devices of a mounted file system cannot change.
.Sh LOADER TUNABLES
These tunables are read when the driver is loaded.
Set them in
.Xr loader.conf 5 ,
or with
.Xr kenv 1
before the module is loaded.
Every limit has a compiled-in maximum, and a larger value is reduced to it.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va vfs.erofs.decoded_cache.enabled
Set to 0 to turn off the cache of decoded extents for all mounts.
The default is 1.
.It Va vfs.erofs.decoded_cache.mount_budget
The size in bytes of the decoded extent cache of one mount.
The maximum, and the default, is 256 KiB.
.It Va vfs.erofs.decoded_cache.global_budget
The size in bytes of the decoded extent caches of all mounts together.
The maximum, and the default, is 512 KiB.
.It Va vfs.erofs.decoded_cache.minimum_decode_work
Extents that are cheaper to decode than this many bytes of input and output
are not cached.
The default is 128 KiB.
.It Va vfs.erofs.decoded_cache.worker_limit
The number of decode threads that each mount starts.
It is reduced to 8 and to the number of CPUs, and is at least 1.
The default is 8.
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.mount_budget
Decompressor contexts are kept in a pool.
This is the largest amount of memory in bytes that the contexts of one mount
may use.
The maximum, and the default, is 32 MiB.
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.global_budget
The same for all mounts together.
The maximum, and the default, is 128 MiB.
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.mount_contexts
The most contexts of one mount and one algorithm.
The maximum, and the default, is 16.
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.global_contexts
The most contexts of all mounts together.
The maximum, and the default, is 64.
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.mount_cached_per_codec
How many idle contexts of each algorithm a mount keeps.
The default is 2.
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.global_cached_per_codec
How many idle contexts of each algorithm all mounts keep together.
The default is 16.
.El
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
The pool of decompressor contexts is described by these read-only variables.
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.mount_budget
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.global_budget
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.mount_contexts
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.global_contexts_limit
The limits in effect, after the tunables above have been applied.
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.resident_bytes
The memory used by all contexts.
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.contexts
The number of allocated contexts.
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.cached
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.borrowed
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.idle
The number of cacheable contexts, of contexts that are in use, and of
contexts that are idle.
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.creations
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.reuses
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.destroys
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.reclaims
Contexts created, taken from the idle list, destroyed, and released because the
system was low on memory.
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.fallbacks
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.exhaustions
.It Va vfs.erofs.stream_pool.waits
Temporary contexts used because the pool was full, requests refused because
of a limit, and sleeps for a context to be released.
.El
.Pp
Every mount has a node
.Va vfs.erofs.mounts\&. Ns Ar id ,
where
.Ar id
is shown by the read-only
.Va mount_id
variable in it.
The node contains:
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It Va dir_ra_bytes
The directory readahead, as the mount option.
It can be written.
.It Va dir_ra_max_bytes
The largest value that
.Va dir_ra_bytes
accepts.
.It Va dir_ra_requests
.It Va dir_ra_blocks
The number of directory readahead requests and of blocks they asked for.
.It Va sync_decompress
.It Va cache_strategy
The mount options of the same name as numbers: 0 to 2 for
.Va sync_decompress
.Pq auto, on, off
and 0 to 2 for
.Va cache_strategy
.Pq disabled, readahead, readaround .
They can be written.
.It Va drop_caches
Write 1 to discard the decoded extents of the mount.
.It Va decoded_cache.hits
.It Va decoded_cache.misses
.It Va decoded_cache.bypasses
.It Va decoded_cache.evictions
.It Va decoded_cache.reclaims
.It Va decoded_cache.waiter_merges
Counters of the decoded extent cache: lookups that found an extent, lookups
that did not, extents that were not eligible for the cache, extents removed to
make room, extents removed because the system was low on memory, and readers
that waited for another reader to decode the same extent.
.It Va decoded_cache.entries
.It Va decoded_cache.bytes
The number of extents in the cache and the memory they use.
.It Va decoded_cache.worker_submissions
.It Va decoded_cache.worker_runs
.It Va decoded_cache.queue_fallbacks
The number of decoding tasks given to the decode threads, the number of tasks
they ran, and the number of times the reading thread decoded the data itself
although
.Cm sync_decompress=off
was set, because there was no queue, the reader was a decode thread, or
queuing the task failed.
.It Va decoded_cache.worker_count
.It Va decoded_cache.worker_cpu_bound
The number of decode threads and whether they are restricted to the online
CPUs.
.It Va decoded_cache.worker_queue_priority
The mount option
.Cm worker_queue_priority .
It can be written.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Mount an image from a disk device:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mount -t erofs /dev/da0s1 /mnt
.Ed
.Pp
Mount an image from a regular file, directly and through
.Xr mdconfig 8 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mount -t erofs rootfs.erofs /mnt
umount /mnt
mdconfig -a -t vnode -f rootfs.erofs -u 0
mount -t erofs /dev/md0 /mnt
.Ed
.Pp
Mount an image that starts 1 MiB into a disk and decode in kernel threads:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mount -t erofs -o fsoffset=1048576,sync_decompress=off /dev/da0 /mnt
.Ed
.Pp
Mount an image whose data is partly on an external device:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mount -t erofs -o device=/dev/md1 /dev/md0 /mnt
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
If a problem with the image is found while the file system is used, the
operation fails with
.Er EINTEGRITY
.Pq Dq Integrity check failed ,
and the place where it was first noticed is written to the system log with
.Xr log 9 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
erofs (/mnt): erofs_readdir_block: invalid dirent array in the block at offset 4096 @ nid 42
.Ed
.Pp
The line names the mount point, the function that found the problem, what was
wrong and, if it concerns one inode, the number of the inode
.Pq Dq nid .
At most five lines a second are written for all mounts together, so a damaged
directory cannot flood the log; every failure is still returned to the
application.
.Pp
The driver does not check that an image is well formed as a whole.
Properties such as sorted directory entries, ordered extent tables and
non-overlapping device ranges can be checked with
.Sy fsck.erofs
from the erofs-utils package.
A violation can make a lookup miss or return another entry, but cannot make
the driver read outside the data it was asked to read.
.Pp
On a kernel with
.Cd "options KDTRACE_HOOKS" ,
the driver provides these
.Xr dtrace 1
probes:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Li erofs:::corrupt Ns Pq Va nid , Va line , Va error
A problem with the image was found, whether or not it was logged.
.Va nid
is the number of the inode or
.Li 0xffffffffffffffff
if the problem does not belong to one inode,
.Va line
the line in the source and
.Va error
the error number.
.It Li erofs:data:map_blocks:enter Ns Pq Va nid , Va la , Va llen , Va flags
.It Li erofs:data:map_blocks:exit Ns Pq Va nid , Va la , Va pa , Va llen , Va plen , Va status
Mapping of a logical range of an inode to its place on the device.
.Va status
holds the error number in its upper 32 bits and the mapping flags in the lower
32 bits.
.It Li erofs:data:read_folio:entry Ns Pq Va nid , Va offset , Va length , Va dir , Va raw
.It Li erofs:data:readahead:entry Ns Pq Va nid , Va start , Va pages , Va raw
A read or a readahead request.
.Va offset
and
.Va length
are in bytes,
.Va start
and
.Va pages
count pages,
.Va dir
is 1 for a directory, and
.Va raw
is 1 if the data is not compressed.
.It Li erofs:inode:fill_inode:entry Ns Pq Va nid , Va blkaddr , Va offset
An inode is read from the device.
.It Li erofs:namei:lookup:entry Ns Pq Va dirnid , Va hash , Va length , Va flags
A name is looked up.
.Va hash
is a 32-bit FNV-1a hash of the name and
.Va flags
are the flags of the lookup.
.El
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dtrace -n 'erofs:::corrupt { printf("nid %u line %d err %d", arg0, arg1, arg2); }'
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtrace 1 ,
.Xr kenv 1 ,
.Xr ffs 4 ,
.Xr loader.conf 5 ,
.Xr mdconfig 8 ,
.Xr mount 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8 ,
.Xr umount 8 ,
.Xr log 9
.Sh HISTORY
EROFS was developed for Linux by Huawei, starting in 2019.
The
.Fx
driver first appeared in
.Fx 16.0 .
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Ruicheng Pan
.Sh BUGS
The driver is read-only.
It does not support every incompatible feature that a future version of
the format may add, nor every encoding used for deduplication.
The Linux mount options
.Cm domain_id
and
.Cm inode_share
are not supported.
.Pp
On a kernel with
.Cd "options WITNESS" ,
the first mount of an image file that is on an
.Xr ffs 4
file system logs a lock order reversal between a vnode of this driver and a
vnode of the file system holding the image.
It is expected: reading holds the vnode lock of this driver while it takes the
lock of the image file, and unmounting holds the lock of the covered directory
while it flushes the vnodes of this driver.
Tests with concurrent reads and unmounts have not shown a deadlock, but there
is no proof that there cannot be one.
